      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Thieme Medical Publishers 333 Seventh Avenue, New York, NY 10001, USA.",
        "AbstractText": "Foreign bodies in the esophagus are commonly seen in emergency medicine. We report here on a very rare case of a working wristwatch in the esophagus, which was successfully extracted by surgical intervention along with primary repair of the perforated cervical esophagus through a transcervical incision. This watch was impacted for 4 days in the cervical esophagus of an adult schizophrenic patient and resulted in cervical esophageal perforation associated with acute deep neck infection."
      },
